        "title": "Culture Shock, Twice",
        "theme": "Erasmus / Culture",
        "length": "short",
        "text": "Most guides for exchange students warn about culture shock when you arrive in a new country. Far fewer mention the second, quieter version that happens when you go home.\n\nDuring my semester abroad, I expected to feel out of place at first, and I did. Simple things, like how loudly people spoke on public transport or how meals were structured, took weeks to feel normal. By the end of my exchange, though, I had adjusted completely. I had new routines, a new favourite café, and a group of international friends who understood exactly what the experience had been like.\n\nWhat surprised me was returning home. I assumed slipping back into my old life would be effortless, since it was, after all, familiar. Instead, I felt strangely disconnected. Friends who hadn't travelled seemed less interested in my stories than I had expected, and small habits I had picked up abroad, like eating dinner later in the evening, suddenly seemed odd to the people around me. I missed the city I had left, even though I had been equally homesick for my own country only months before.\n\nTalking to other returning exchange students, I realised this experience, sometimes called 'reverse culture shock', is extremely common, even though almost nobody warns you about it beforehand. It usually fades within a few weeks, as you reconnect with old friends and settle back into familiar routines, but while it lasts, it can feel confusing and oddly lonely.\n\nIf I could give one piece of advice to future exchange students, it would be this: prepare for the goodbye as carefully as you prepared for the hello. The adjustment does not end when you land back in your home country; in many ways, a second, smaller adjustment is just beginning."
